const e=[{id:"pe-l-evidence-relevance",moduleId:"m10-evidence",title:"What a court is allowed to listen to",minutes:20,summary:"A court does not hear everything that is true. It hears a defined list of things, and the Evidence Act 1950 is that list.",plants:["pe-c-ev-01","pe-c-ev-02","pe-c-ev-03","pe-c-ev-04"],prepares:["pe-p-evidence-01"],reading:[{bookId:"mazlina-law-of-evidence",where:"the opening chapters on relevancy, and the chapter on admissions"}],source:"Evidence Act 1950 (Act 56), s 5 and the relevancy provisions in Part I; the provisions on oral evidence and on admissions.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"The Act is free at lom.agc.gov.my. Section 5 is quoted in substance, not word for word. The hearsay and admission rules are described generally on purpose — read the sections themselves in Parts I and II before relying on them.",plate:"earshot",plateCaption:"What reaches the bench and what does not. The question is never whether evidence is interesting but which section lets it in.",readMinutes:6,sections:[{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-relevance-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A judge stops a witness mid-sentence. It looks like a technicality and it is not.",h:"The thing everyone gets wrong about trials",body:[{t:"p",text:"You read a report of a case. A witness starts to say something and the judge stops them. It looks like a trick, or a technicality."},{t:"p",text:"It is neither. A court does not listen to everything that might be true. It listens to a defined set of things, and the rest never gets through the door."},{t:"p",text:"This lesson is about that door."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-relevance-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Facts in issue are what must be proved for the claim to succeed — the pleadings fix them.",h:"Facts in issue",body:[{t:"p",text:"Take a small case. Devi says her neighbour Chong reversed his car into her gate and broke it. She wants RM1,200."},{t:"p",text:"Only two things are really in dispute. Did Chong hit the gate? What did the damage cost to put right?"},{t:"p",text:"Those are the **facts in issue** — the facts the case turns on. Everything the court hears has to earn its place by connecting to one of them."},{t:"example",text:"That Chong is rude to people at the surau is not a fact in issue. It may be perfectly true. It still does not help decide who broke the gate."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-relevance-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The Act is a closed list: facts in issue, facts it declares relevant, and nothing else.",h:"Relevancy is set by the Act, not by common sense",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Evidence Act 1950 lists the kinds of fact that may be proved. It is a code, and it works as a closed list."},{t:"rule",text:"Evidence may be given of facts in issue and of such other facts as the Act declares to be relevant, and of no others.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 5"},{t:"p",text:"So the real question is never 'is this relevant?' in the everyday sense. It is 'which section makes this relevant?'"},{t:"p",text:"The list is generous. It covers the cause and effect of a fact in issue, motive, preparation, and what a person did before and after the event."},{t:"caution",text:"An English textbook starts from the opposite end: relevant evidence comes in unless a rule keeps it out. Malaysia inverts that. If you cannot name a section, the evidence stays out."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"The question at the door",caption:"An English textbook starts from the opposite end: relevant evidence comes in unless a rule keeps it out. Malaysia inverts that. The list is generous, but it is a list.",alt:"A question — can you name the section of the Evidence Act that makes this fact relevant? — branching two ways. Yes: it may be proved. No: it does not come in, because there is no general route for evidence that merely seems relevant.",question:"Can you name the section that makes this fact relevant?",branches:[{cond:"yes",label:"It may be proved",note:"the Act declares it relevant"},{cond:"no",label:"It does not come in",note:"there is no 'seems relevant' route"}]}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-relevance-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Oral evidence must be direct — a witness speaks to what that witness perceived.",h:"Hearsay, in outline",body:[{t:"p",text:"Devi's cousin was not there that day. He wants to tell the court that a passer-by told him Chong hit the gate."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Devi's cousin was not there that day. He wants to tell the judge that a passer-by told him he saw Chong hit the gate. Will the court listen?",options:["Generally not, because the man who saw it is not here","Yes, he is willing to swear to it","Yes, if the passer-by cannot be found"],answer:0,reveal:"Generally not, and it feels unfair at first. Nobody is calling the cousin a liar. It is that only the passer-by can be asked how far away he was, or how sure."},{t:"p",text:"The court will generally not take that. The passer-by is the one who saw it, and the passer-by is not in the room to be questioned."},{t:"rule",text:"Oral evidence must be direct. A witness speaks to what that witness saw, heard or otherwise perceived.",source:"Evidence Act 1950"},{t:"p",text:"A second-hand account, offered to prove that what was said is true, is called **hearsay**."},{t:"caution",text:"There are real exceptions, and in Malaysia they are statutory. Never write that hearsay is never admissible. Write that it does not come in unless a provision lets it in, then go and find the provision."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-relevance-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"An admission can be proved against the person who made it.",h:"Admissions: the words that come back",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two weeks after the gate broke, Chong sent Devi a message: 'Sorry lah, I will pay for the gate this month.'"},{t:"p",text:"That message is the best thing Devi has. A statement by a party that points to a fact against their own interest is an **admission**."},{t:"rule",text:"An admission may be proved against the person who made it.",source:"Evidence Act 1950"},{t:"p",text:"This is why lawyers tell clients to stop explaining themselves on WhatsApp. The apology is evidence and it does not go away."},{t:"caution",text:"An admission does not end a case. It is evidence, and the maker may explain it. In a criminal case an admission by an accused is a confession, and confessions are governed by stricter conditions."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-relevance-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"'Ruled inadmissible' rarely means the truth was hidden.",h:"How to read a trial report from now on",body:[{t:"p",text:"When a report says evidence was 'ruled inadmissible', it rarely means the truth was hidden."},{t:"p",text:"It means nobody could point to the rule that lets the court use it. That is a different complaint, and a much smaller one."}]}],quiz:[{id:"pe-q-evr-1",q:"Devi sues Chong over a broken gate. Which of these is a fact in issue?",options:["Whether Chong is disliked in the neighbourhood","Whether Chong has been sued before","Whether Chong's car hit the gate","Whether Devi can afford the repair"],answer:2,why:"A fact in issue is one the case turns on. The others may be true and still change nothing about who broke the gate.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 5"},{id:"pe-q-evr-2",q:"Under the Evidence Act 1950, what is the right question to ask about a piece of evidence?",options:["Is there a rule that excludes it?","Does it feel important to the case?","Which section of the Act makes this relevant?","Would an ordinary person want to hear it?"],answer:2,why:"The Act is a closed list, so relevancy has to be found in a section. Starting from the English question, whether anything excludes it, gets the system backwards.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, Part I; relevancy is a closed list"},{id:"pe-q-evr-3",q:"A witness wants to repeat what an absent passer-by told him, to prove that it happened. What is that?",options:["Hearsay","An admission","Circumstantial evidence","Expert evidence"],answer:0,why:"It is second-hand and offered for its truth, and the person who saw the event cannot be questioned. Calling it circumstantial evidence confuses the source with the strength.",source:"Evidence Act 1950; oral evidence must be direct"},{id:"pe-q-evr-4",q:"Chong messages Devi: 'Sorry, I will pay for the gate this month.' What is the message worth to her?",options:["It is an admission and may be proved against him","Nothing, because it was not made in court","Nothing, because an apology is not a promise","It settles the case and ends the trial"],answer:0,why:"A party's own words pointing against their interest may be proved against them. But an admission is evidence, not the end of the case, and he may still explain it.",source:"Evidence Act 1950; the provisions on admissions"},{id:"pe-q-evr-5",q:"A news report says a judge ruled some evidence inadmissible. What does that usually mean?",options:["The judge decided it was untrue","The witness was found to be lying","The evidence was obtained illegally","No rule allowed the court to use it"],answer:3,why:"Admissibility is about whether a rule lets the material in, not about whether it is true. Reading it as a finding of falsehood is the usual mistake.",source:"Evidence Act 1950; admissibility is decided by the Act, not by whether the material is true"}]},{id:"pe-l-evidence-proof",moduleId:"m10-evidence",title:"Who has to prove it, and how sure the judge must be",minutes:20,summary:"A court starts from a blank page. Which side has to fill it, and how convincingly, decides more cases than any clever argument.",plants:["pe-c-ev-05","pe-c-ev-06","pe-c-ev-07","pe-c-ev-08"],prepares:["pe-p-evidence-01"],reading:[{bookId:"malaysian-law-of-evidence-student",where:"the chapters on burden and standard of proof, and on presumptions"}],source:"Evidence Act 1950 (Act 56), ss 101, 102, 106 and 114 illustration (g); the standards of proof as ordinarily stated.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Sections 101, 102, 106 and 114 are quoted in substance and should be read in the current text at lom.agc.gov.my. The standard of proof for fraud alleged in civil proceedings has been litigated in Malaysia; check the current authority before advancing either standard.",plate:"who-proves",plateCaption:"Who has to prove it, and how sure the judge must be. Two separate questions that answers routinely run together.",readMinutes:7,sections:[{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-proof-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A court starts with a blank page, and nothing goes on it because somebody said it loudly.",h:"The default is that nothing happened",body:[{t:"p",text:"A court begins with a blank page. Nothing goes on it because somebody said it loudly, or said it first."},{t:"p",text:"Somebody has to fill that page. Working out which side that is may be the most useful thing in this module."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-proof-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Whoever asserts a fact his case depends on must prove it.",h:"Whoever asserts, must prove",body:[{t:"p",text:"Kumar says he lent his neighbour Hafiz RM3,000 in cash. Hafiz says it was a gift for his wedding. Kumar sues."},{t:"rule",text:"Whoever wants a court to give judgment on a right or liability that depends on facts he asserts must prove that those facts exist.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 101"},{t:"p",text:"So Kumar must prove the loan. If neither side calls a single witness, Kumar loses."},{t:"rule",text:"The burden of proof lies on the person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 102"},{t:"p",text:"That is a handy test. Picture an empty trial, and ask who walks out disappointed."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-proof-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The legal burden stays put; the evidential burden moves as the evidence goes in.",h:"Two burdens, and why they get mixed up",body:[{t:"p",text:"Kumar produces a bank transfer slip and a message from Hafiz saying 'I will return it next month'. Hafiz is now in trouble unless he answers."},{t:"p",text:"The need to answer has moved across the table. The risk of losing has not."},{t:"list",items:["The **legal burden** is the risk of losing if the judge is not persuaded. It sits with one side from start to finish.","The **evidential burden** is the practical need to put up some evidence now. It moves back and forth as the trial goes on."]},{t:"caution",text:"'The burden shifted to the defendant' is the sloppiest sentence in this subject. Say which burden you mean. The legal burden moves only where a statute moves it."},{t:"compare",title:"Two burdens. Only one of them moves.",left:{h:"The legal burden",items:["The risk of losing if the judge is not persuaded.","It sits with one side from the start of the case to the end of it.","It moves only where a statute moves it.","This is the one that decides who wins on an even balance."]},right:{h:"The evidential burden",items:["The practical need to put up some evidence now.","It moves back and forth as the case goes on.","Kumar's transfer slip moved it to Hafiz.","Moving it does not change who loses if nobody persuades the judge."]}}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-proof-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Balance of probabilities in a civil case; beyond reasonable doubt in a criminal one.",h:"How sure is sure enough",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are two standards, and everything turns on which one you are under."},{t:"rule",text:"In a civil case the claimant proves his case on the balance of probabilities. More likely than not is enough.",source:"Standard doctrine under the Evidence Act 1950, Part II"},{t:"rule",text:"In a criminal case the prosecution must prove guilt beyond reasonable doubt.",source:"Standard doctrine under the Evidence Act 1950, Part II"},{t:"p",text:"The gap between the two is wide. A judge may think it fairly likely that the accused did it and must still acquit."},{t:"diagram",kind:"spectrum",title:"The two standards, on one scale",caption:"The gap between them is wide. A judge may think it fairly likely that the accused did it and must still acquit. The same facts can lose a prosecution and win a civil suit.",alt:"A scale running from no confidence at all to certainty. The balance of probabilities sits just past halfway, and is the civil standard. Beyond reasonable doubt sits near the far end, and is the criminal standard.",from:"not persuaded at all",to:"certain",marks:[{at:.52,label:"balance of probabilities — civil"},{at:.88,label:"beyond reasonable doubt — criminal"}]},{t:"example",text:"A man is acquitted of hurting his neighbour. The neighbour then sues him for the injury and wins. Nothing has gone wrong. The second case needed less."},{t:"caution",text:"Fraud alleged in a civil case is a known wrinkle. Malaysian courts have gone back and forth over whether it needs the criminal standard. Check the current position before you rely on either view."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-proof-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A fact especially within your knowledge is yours to explain.",h:"Some facts are yours to explain",body:[{t:"rule",text:"When a fact is especially within the knowledge of a person, the burden of proving that fact is on him.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 106"},{t:"example",text:"A man is stopped riding a motorcycle without plates. Where he got the machine, and whether he holds a licence, are things only he can readily explain."},{t:"caution",text:"Section 106 is the most over-used provision in the Act. It does not move the main burden. The prosecution still proves its case, and s 106 reaches only a narrow fact peculiarly within one person's knowledge."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-proof-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A court may presume that evidence withheld would have been unfavourable.",h:"The evidence nobody produced",body:[{t:"rule",text:"A court may presume that evidence which could be produced, and is not produced, would be unfavourable to the party who withholds it.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 114 illustration (g)"},{t:"p",text:"May, not must. The party asking for the inference has to show that the witness or document was available and mattered."},{t:"caution",text:"A gap is not the same as a withholding. If the missing witness would only repeat what is already proved, no inference follows."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-evidence-proof-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"'Acquitted' does not mean innocent — it means the prosecution did not reach its standard.",h:"Reading the news again",body:[{t:"p",text:"'Acquitted' does not mean a court found the person innocent. It means the prosecution did not reach its standard."},{t:"p",text:"'She failed to prove it' does not mean she lied. It means she carried a burden and fell short."},{t:"p",text:"Once you see this, half of what people argue about after a verdict stops making sense. That is progress."}]}],quiz:[{id:"pe-q-evp-1",q:"Kumar says he lent Hafiz RM3,000. Hafiz says it was a gift. Neither calls any evidence. What happens?",options:["Hafiz loses, because he asserted the gift","The judge splits it between them","Kumar loses, because he asserted the loan","The case is adjourned for evidence"],answer:2,why:"Section 102 puts the burden on whoever would fail on an empty record. Kumar wants judgment, so the blank page defeats him.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 102"},{id:"pe-q-evp-2",q:"Which burden moves back and forth during a trial?",options:["The legal burden","The evidential burden","Both, together","Neither; burdens are fixed by the pleadings"],answer:1,why:"The legal burden is the risk of not persuading the court and stays put. Saying only that 'the burden shifted' hides which one you mean.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 ss 101 and 102"},{id:"pe-q-evp-3",q:"A man is acquitted of assault, then loses a civil claim brought by the same victim. Is that a contradiction?",options:["No. The civil case needed a lower standard of proof","Yes. One court must be wrong","No, but only because different judges heard them","Yes, unless new evidence appeared"],answer:0,why:"Beyond reasonable doubt is a much higher bar than more likely than not. Treating an acquittal as a finding of innocence is the trap.",source:"Standard classification; the criminal and civil standards of proof differ"},{id:"pe-q-evp-4",q:"What does s 106 of the Evidence Act 1950 do?",options:["Puts the burden of a fact especially within someone's knowledge on that person","Moves the whole burden to the accused","Lets a court presume guilt from silence","Sets the standard of proof in criminal cases"],answer:0,why:"It reaches one narrow fact only. Using it to relieve the prosecution of proving its case is the standard misuse of the section.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 106"},{id:"pe-q-evp-5",q:"A party could have called an available and material witness and did not. What may the court do?",options:["It must presume the evidence would have been unfavourable","It must strike out that party's case","It may presume the evidence would have been unfavourable to that party","It must draw no inference at all"],answer:2,why:"Illustration (g) to s 114 is permissive. Treating it as automatic is the mistake, and so is invoking it for a witness who would add nothing.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 114 illustration (g)"}]},{id:"l-evidence",moduleId:"m10-evidence",title:"Relevancy is the gate",minutes:30,summary:"The Evidence Act is a closed list of what may be proved, which makes it a different system from the English law of admissibility.",plants:[],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"malaysian-law-of-evidence-student",where:"the chapters on relevancy and on burden of proof"},{bookId:"sarkar-evidence-malaysia",where:"as the reference work, section by section, when you need depth on one provision"}],source:"Evidence Act 1950 (Act 56), Parts I and II; ss 101, 102, 103, 106, 114.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read the relevancy sections and ss 101–106 and 114 in a current reprint of Act 56 — the section numbers here are the stable ones, but the confession provisions and their case law should be checked directly rather than taken from this summary.",plate:"gate",plateCaption:"The gate at section 5. A closed list of what may be proved is a different system from a general rule of relevance with exceptions.",readMinutes:12,sections:[{id:"sec-l-evidence-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A neighbour wants to tell the court the accused has a bad temper. Can she?",h:"A witness with something to say",body:[{t:"p",text:"A man is on trial for injuring his neighbour in a quarrel. A woman from three doors down wants to tell the court that the accused has always had a bad temper. Will the judge let her?"},{t:"predict",prompt:"The neighbour wants to tell the court that the accused has always had a bad temper. Before you read on — will the judge let her?",options:["Yes, if the judge thinks it helps to decide the case","Only if a section of the Evidence Act makes that fact relevant","No — what a person is generally like is never evidence"],answer:1,reveal:"A section, or nothing. The first answer is how a trial looks from outside, and it is roughly where English evidence starts. Relevant material goes in unless a rule keeps it out. The Act turns that round. Nothing comes in unless a section lets it in. The third answer overshoots. Character is relevant in Malaysia, but only in narrow circumstances that a section sets out."},{t:"p",text:"In Malaysia the answer does not come from a general sense of what is fair. It comes from asking which section of the Evidence Act makes that fact relevant. If no section does, it does not come in at all."}]},{id:"sec-l-evidence-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The Act is a closed list: evidence of facts in issue and facts it declares relevant, and of no others.",h:"Why an English textbook misleads here",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Evidence Act 1950 descends from the Indian Evidence Act of 1872, drafted by Stephen, and it is a code. That means it sets out the whole of the subject in its own terms, rather than restating what the judges had decided."},{t:"p",text:"It is not organised the way English evidence is organised, and reading it as though it were will lead you astray."},{t:"p",text:"The English approach asks whether a piece of evidence is admissible. It starts from a general principle that relevant evidence goes in, unless some rule of exclusion pushes it back out again."},{t:"p",text:"The Act turns that whole arrangement upside down. Part I sets out the categories of fact that are relevant, and nothing outside those categories may be proved at all."},{t:"rule",text:"Evidence may be given of facts in issue and of such other facts as are declared relevant by the Act, and of no others.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 (Act 56), Part I"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the Act is a closed list of what may be proved. Nothing gets in on general grounds of fairness or common sense."},{t:"compare",title:"Two ways of arranging the same question",left:{h:"The English arrangement",items:["Start from a general principle: relevant evidence is admissible.","Then apply exclusionary rules that keep particular things out.","Hearsay is a rule of exclusion with a list of exceptions hanging off it.","The question is: **is there a rule that excludes this?**"]},right:{h:"The Evidence Act 1950",items:["Part I declares the categories of fact that are relevant.","Nothing outside those categories may be proved at all.","There is no general hearsay rule. Statements by absent persons are simply not made relevant, except where a section makes them so.","The question is: **which section makes this fact relevant?**"]}},{t:"caution",text:"So the question is never simply “is this relevant?” in the ordinary sense of the word. The question you have to answer is “which section of the Act makes this fact relevant?”. If you cannot name a section, the evidence does not come in, and an argument about relevance with no section behind it has missed the structure of the whole Act."}]},{id:"sec-l-evidence-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Know the shape of the relevancy sections rather than holding all of them at once.",h:"The relevancy sections",body:[{t:"p",text:"Part I of the Act works through those categories one at a time. You do not need to hold them all in your head at once, but you should know the shape of the list and be able to find the right one."},{t:"list",items:["Facts forming part of the same transaction — the res gestae provision.","Facts which are the occasion, cause or effect of facts in issue.","Motive, preparation, and the conduct of a party before or after the events in question.","Facts that are necessary in order to explain or to introduce a relevant fact.","Admissions made by a party, and confessions as a special class of admission.","Statements made by persons who cannot themselves be called as witnesses.","Opinions of experts, on science, art, foreign law, handwriting or fingerprints.","Character, which is relevant in narrow and different circumstances in civil and criminal cases."]},{t:"p",text:"**Relevancy** is the gate, then, and a fact is admissible only because some section of the Act makes it relevant."},{t:"p",text:"Hearsay deserves a note, because the Act handles it differently from English law. **Hearsay** is a statement made outside court, offered to prove that what it said was true."},{t:"p",text:"There is no single hearsay rule here with a list of exceptions hanging off it. Statements by absent persons are simply not made relevant, except where a section makes them so, which reaches a similar place by a different route."}]},{id:"sec-l-evidence-3",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Whoever asserts must prove — and the legal burden does not shift the way the evidential one does.",h:"Who has to prove what",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Rosnah sues her former tenant for RM2,400 of unpaid rent. He says that he paid her in cash and never got a receipt for it. Who has to prove what here?"},{t:"p",text:"Part II of the Act deals with proof, and the burden provisions are the ones you will use most often of everything in it."},{t:"rule",text:"Whoever desires any court to give judgment as to any legal right or liability, dependent on the existence of facts which he asserts, must prove that those facts exist.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 101"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: if you want the court to decide something in your favour, and it turns on a fact, then you prove that fact. So Rosnah has to prove that the rent fell due, and the tenant has to prove that he paid it."},{t:"rule",text:"The burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 102"},{t:"p",text:"Section 103 puts the burden on any particular fact on the person who wants the court to believe that fact exists. Section 106 puts on a person the burden of proving a fact that is especially within their own knowledge."},{t:"p",text:"That last one comes up constantly in criminal cases, and it is regularly pushed further by prosecutors than it will actually go."},{t:"caution",text:"Keep the two kinds of burden apart in your mind. The **legal burden** is the risk of losing if a fact is not proved, and it does not shift. The **evidential burden** is the burden of putting enough evidence on a point to make it a live issue, and that one does shift. Saying that the burden shifted to the accused, without saying which burden, is the standard error here — the legal burden in a criminal case stays with the prosecution throughout."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Puan Rosnah sues for RM2,400 of unpaid rent. Her former tenant says he paid her in cash. Who has to prove the payment?",options:["The tenant — payment is the fact he is asserting","Puan Rosnah — she started the case, so every fact in it is hers to prove","Neither — the court simply decides whose account is more likely"],answer:0,why:"The burden on a particular fact sits on whoever wants the court to believe that fact. Rosnah proves the rent fell due, and the tenant proves he paid it. The first answer confuses carrying the case with carrying every fact inside it. The third skips the burden provisions altogether, and they are the part of the Act you will reach for most often."}]},{id:"sec-l-evidence-4",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Illustration (g) lets a court presume withheld evidence would have been unfavourable, but not automatically.",h:"Presumptions, and the missing witness",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Act separates facts a court **shall** presume from facts it **may** presume. A **presumption** is a direction about what the court may or must infer from something."},{t:"p",text:"The discretionary ones in section 114 are argued most often, and one of its illustrations has taken on a life of its own."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 114 illustration (g): the court may presume that evidence which could be and is not produced would, if produced, be unfavourable to the person who withholds it.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 114(g)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: if you could have produced a witness or a document and you did not, the court is allowed to conclude that it would not have helped you."},{t:"example",text:"A landlord sues over damage to a rented room and relies on his own account of it. The handyman who inspected the room the next morning is available to give evidence, and is never called. The court may hold that gap against the landlord."},{t:"p",text:"The presumption is permissive rather than automatic, which matters a great deal in argument. The court asks whether the missing witness was genuinely material and genuinely available before drawing anything at all from the gap."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"When illustration (g) actually bites",caption:"The presumption is permissive, not automatic. An answer that treats the inference as following from absence alone has skipped both questions the court actually asks.",alt:"A question — was the missing evidence both available to that party and material? — branching two ways. Available and material: the court may presume it would have been unfavourable, though it is never bound to. Merely cumulative of what is already proved, or not that party's to call: no inference arises.",question:"Was the missing evidence available to that party, and material?",branches:[{cond:"available, and material",label:"The court may presume against them",note:"may, not must — s 114 is discretionary"},{cond:"cumulative, or not theirs to call",label:"No inference arises",note:"repeating proved evidence attracts nothing"}]},{t:"caution",text:"Failing to call a witness whose evidence would merely repeat what is already proved does not attract the inference. The provision is about evidence that was withheld, not about evidence that was simply incomplete."}]},{id:"sec-l-evidence-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Confessions are a distinct class of admission with conditions of their own.",h:"Confessions",body:[{t:"p",text:"Confessions are admissions made in criminal cases, and the Act treats them as a distinct class with conditions of their own."},{t:"p",text:"A confession is irrelevant if it appears to have been caused by an inducement, a threat or a promise. That must come from a person in authority, and must be enough to give the accused grounds for supposing he would gain an advantage or escape something bad."},{t:"p",text:"This is one of the most heavily litigated parts of the Act and the case law is enormous. Read the sections themselves before any commentary, and be careful with older authority."}]},{id:"sec-l-evidence-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Take a fact you want to prove and go and find the section that lets you prove it.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are no flashcards or problems for this module yet. The single most useful exercise is to take a fact you would want to prove, and go and find the section that makes it relevant."},{t:"p",text:"Doing that ten times teaches the Act better than reading a chapter about it. Start with the neighbour who wants to talk about the accused's temper."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qev1",q:"What is the right question to ask about a piece of evidence under the Act?",options:["Is this relevant in the ordinary sense","Which section makes this relevant","Would it be fair to admit this","Does this help prove anything"],answer:1,why:"The Act is a closed list. If you cannot name a section, the evidence does not go in.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, Part I; relevancy is a closed list"},{id:"qev2",q:"Which of these is a relevancy provision under the Act?",options:["Facts forming part of the same transaction","The hearsay rule at common law","The best evidence rule","A judge's discretion to exclude unfair evidence"],answer:0,why:"That is the res gestae provision, and the relevancy sections are where an answer should start.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 6"},{id:"qev3",q:"Which burden does not shift?",options:["The evidential burden","Both of them shift","Neither of them shifts","The legal burden"],answer:3,why:"Saying 'the burden shifted' without saying which one is the standard imprecision here.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 ss 101 and 102"},{id:"qev4",q:"When does illustration (g) to section 114 not apply?",options:["Where the witness is out of the country","Where the party is the accused","Where the missing evidence would only repeat what is already proved","Where the evidence is a document rather than a witness"],answer:2,why:"The provision is about evidence that was withheld and would have added something.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 114(g)"},{id:"qev5",q:"Who must prove a fact he asserts, where a right or liability depends on it?",options:["The person who wants the court to give judgment on it","Whichever party can get at the evidence more easily","The plaintiff, always","The defendant, once a prima facie case is shown"],answer:0,why:"Section 101, which is the legal burden.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 101"}]},{id:"l-hearsay-danger",moduleId:"m10-evidence",title:"What is lost when the speaker is absent",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Hearsay is not kept out because second-hand accounts are always false. It is kept out because the person who actually saw the thing is not there to be tested.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 (Act 56), s 5; the provisions of the Act on oral evidence, on statements by persons who cannot be called as witnesses, and on documentary evidence.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for hearsay, for oral evidence, for statements by persons who cannot be called as witnesses, or for documentary evidence on purpose; confirm each in the current Evidence Act 1950 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"evidence-act-1950",where:"section 5 first, then the Part I sections that make statements by absent persons relevant"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-hearsay-danger-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A sergeant wants to repeat what a bystander told him. The bystander is not in the room.",h:"The officer who was not there",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Firdaus is on trial in Ipoh for snatch theft. The only witness the prosecution has is Sergeant Lim."},{t:"p",text:"Sergeant Lim reached the scene four minutes after the snatch. He did not see it. He wants to tell the court what a bystander told him there."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Sergeant Lim wants to repeat what the bystander said, to prove that Firdaus did it. Will the court take that?",options:["Generally not, because the bystander is the one who saw it","Yes, the sergeant is a police officer and is under oath","Yes, but only if the bystander has since died"],answer:0,reveal:"Generally not. Nobody is calling Sergeant Lim a liar. The problem is that the bystander cannot be asked a single question. Death does not open the door by itself either. It matters only where a section makes such a statement relevant."},{t:"p",text:"The sergeant is honest. That is not the difficulty. The difficulty is that the person who saw the theft is not in the room."}]},{id:"sec-l-hearsay-danger-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Cross-examination attacks perception, memory, honesty and wording. An absent speaker escapes all four.",h:"The four things you cannot test",body:[{t:"p",text:"Put a witness in the box and there are four separate things you can attack. An absent speaker is safe from every one of them."},{t:"table",caption:"What cross-examination reaches, and what an absent speaker escapes",columns:["The risk","The question you would ask","Why the absent speaker escapes it"],rows:[["Perception","How far away were you? Was it dark?","Nobody can ask the bystander where he was standing."],["Memory","When did you first tell anyone this?","The gap between seeing and speaking goes untested."],["Honesty","Do you know the accused? Do you owe anyone a favour?","No oath was taken and no motive is exposed."],["Choice of words","You said he grabbed it. Do you mean he pulled, or took?","An ambiguous phrase is passed on as if it were plain."]],alt:"A table of four risks in second-hand evidence. Perception: nobody can ask the absent speaker where he stood or how dark it was. Memory: the gap between seeing and speaking goes untested. Honesty: no oath was taken and no motive is exposed. Choice of words: an ambiguous phrase is passed on as though it were plain."},{t:"p",text:"A witness in the box also takes an oath. The judge watches how the answers come out. None of that reaches the bystander."},{t:"p",text:"So the objection is not that the report is false. The objection is that it cannot be checked."},{t:"caution",text:"This is why an offer to prove the bystander was reliable does not help. The rule is not about that speaker. It is about the machinery that is missing."}]},{id:"sec-l-hearsay-danger-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Words repeated to show they were said are ordinary evidence. Words repeated to show they were true are hearsay.",h:"Ask what the statement is offered to prove",body:[{t:"p",text:"The same sentence can be hearsay in one case and ordinary evidence in the next. It depends on why you want it in."},{t:"p",text:"Ask one question. Are you asking the court to believe what the words said, or only that the words were said?"},{t:"example",text:"Cik Suria sues a builder. She says she warned him about a leaking pipe on 3 March. Repeating the warning to prove she gave notice is fine. Repeating it to prove the pipe was leaking is hearsay."},{t:"list",items:["**Words as the act itself.** An offer, an acceptance, a threat, a defamatory sentence. The words are the event the case is about.","**Words to show notice.** What matters is that the hearer was told, not that the message was true.","**Words to show a state of mind.** What a person said about their own fear or intention can show the state they were in."]},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A guard says he opened the gate because the driver told him the manager had approved it. It is offered to explain why he opened it. Is that hearsay?",options:["No, because it explains the guard's conduct rather than proving approval","Yes, because the driver is not in court","Only if the manager denies approving anything"],answer:0,why:"The court is not being asked to believe that the manager approved anything. It is being asked to see why the guard acted. Offer the same words to prove approval and they become hearsay at once."}]},{id:"sec-l-hearsay-danger-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"There is no single hearsay rule here with exceptions hanging off it, only a closed list of what a section makes relevant.",h:"How the Act arranges it",body:[{t:"p",text:"English law states a rule against hearsay and then hangs a long list of exceptions on it. The Act does not work that way."},{t:"rule",text:"Evidence may be given in any suit or proceeding of the existence or non-existence of every fact in issue and of such other facts as are declared to be relevant by this Act, and of no others.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 5"},{t:"p",text:"So a statement made out of court is not pushed back out by an exclusionary rule. It simply never becomes relevant, unless a section makes it relevant."},{t:"p",text:"Several sections do. The Act makes certain statements relevant where the maker cannot be called, and it has its own regime for records and documents."},{t:"p",text:"That route reaches roughly where the English exceptions reach. It arrives by the opposite road, and the difference shows up in how you write the answer."},{t:"caution",text:"Never write that hearsay is never admissible. Write that it does not come in unless a provision lets it in. Then go and name the provision."},{t:"p",text:"This lesson gives you no section numbers for those routes on purpose. Look them up in the current Act and write the numbers into your own notes."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-confessions",moduleId:"m10-evidence",title:"A confession, and what must be true before it counts",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A confession is the strongest thing a prosecution can hold, which is why the Act puts conditions on using it. The first question is never what the accused said, but how it came to be said.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 (Act 56), the provisions on admissions and confessions in Part I; Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), ss 28A and 117.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for confessions, for inducement, threat or promise, for confessions made to a police officer, or for statements made in police custody on purpose; confirm each in the current Evidence Act 1950 and Criminal Procedure Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"evidence-act-1950",where:"the confession provisions in Part I, read straight through before any textbook on them"},{statuteId:"criminal-procedure-code",where:"section 28A and section 117, which are what the hours after an arrest are argued against"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-confessions-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Encik Hafiz signed a statement after an officer told him things would go easier. That sentence is the whole issue.",h:"Three in the morning at the station",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Hafiz is arrested in Seremban over a house-breaking. He is taken to the station at eleven at night."},{t:"p",text:"At three in the morning he signs a statement admitting he was inside the house. An officer had told him things would go easier if he cooperated."},{t:"predict",prompt:"The prosecution wants the signed statement before the court. What does the court ask first?",options:["Whether the statement was made voluntarily","Whether what Hafiz wrote is true","Whether Hafiz had a lawyer with him"],answer:0,reveal:"Voluntariness comes first. Truth is not the entry test. An induced statement is kept out even if every word of it turns out to be accurate. The lawyer question matters a great deal, and it is a separate one, taken up later in this lesson."},{t:"p",text:"Notice the order. The court does not begin with whether the statement is true. It begins with how it was obtained."}]},{id:"sec-l-confessions-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Not every damaging sentence is a confession, and the conditions in this lesson attach only to confessions.",h:"What counts as a confession",body:[{t:"p",text:"An admission is any statement by a party pointing to a fact against their own interest. A confession is narrower than that."},{t:"rule",text:"A confession is a statement made by an accused person which suggests the inference that he committed the offence charged.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, the provisions on admissions and confessions in Part I. No section number is stated here; see the verify note for this lesson."},{t:"p",text:"So the statement has to point at guilt. A statement that admits one small damaging fact is an admission and nothing more."},{t:"example",text:"Hafiz says he was on that street at midnight. That places him nearby and it hurts him. It is not a confession, because being on a street is not the offence."},{t:"p",text:"The line matters because of what follows from it. The conditions in the next section attach to confessions. An ordinary admission does not carry them."},{t:"caution",text:"A mixed statement causes trouble. A man may admit taking the goods and add that he believed they were his. Courts do not simply keep the damaging half and drop the rest."}]},{id:"sec-l-confessions-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"An inducement, threat or promise from a person in authority spoils a confession, whether or not it is true.",h:"Voluntariness, and who can spoil it",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Act makes a confession irrelevant where it appears to have been caused by an inducement, a threat or a promise."},{t:"p",text:"Three things have to line up before that bites."},{t:"list",items:["**An inducement, threat or promise.** Something offered or held over the accused, not merely a long and unpleasant night.","**From a person in authority.** A police officer or a prosecutor is one. A friend in the next cell usually is not.","**Enough to give grounds for supposing an advantage.** The accused must reasonably suppose he gains something, or escapes something, in the proceedings against him."]},{t:"p",text:"The word appears is doing real work there. The court asks how the confession looks on the material before it. The accused does not have to prove a threat."},{t:"example",text:"Telling Hafiz that things go easier if he cooperates is a promise of advantage from a person in authority. That is the classic shape of the objection."},{t:"p",text:"Where voluntariness is disputed, the court holds a trial within a trial. It hears evidence about how the statement was taken, before deciding whether to admit the statement itself."},{t:"caution",text:"This is one of the most heavily litigated parts of the Act, and older authority has been overtaken. Read the sections themselves before any commentary. Check the date of anything you rely on."}]},{id:"sec-l-confessions-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Section 28A of the Criminal Procedure Code sets out the rights on arrest, and section 117 controls how long detention may run.",h:"What the Code gives a person in custody",body:[{t:"p",text:"Voluntariness is not argued in the abstract. It is argued against what happened in the hours after arrest. The Criminal Procedure Code governs those hours."},{t:"rule",text:"A person arrested shall be informed as soon as may be of the grounds of his arrest. He shall be allowed to communicate with a relative or friend, and to consult a legal practitioner of his choice.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code, s 28A"},{t:"p",text:"The section also restricts questioning until the arrested person has had that consultation. The exceptions to that are set out in the section itself."},{t:"rule",text:"A police officer who wants to detain an arrested person beyond twenty-four hours must produce him before a Magistrate and apply for a remand order.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code, s 117"},{t:"table",caption:"Where to look when a confession is challenged",columns:["What is being attacked","Where the rule sits"],rows:[["Whether the statement was voluntary","The confession provisions of the Evidence Act 1950"],["Whether he was told why he was arrested","Criminal Procedure Code, s 28A"],["Whether he was allowed to call a lawyer","Criminal Procedure Code, s 28A"],["Whether the detention itself was lawful","Criminal Procedure Code, s 117"],["Whether a statement to a police officer may be used at all","A separate provision of the Code, amended more than once"]],alt:"A table pairing each line of attack on a confession with where the rule sits. Voluntariness sits in the confession provisions of the Evidence Act 1950. Being told the grounds of arrest and being allowed a lawyer both sit in section 28A of the Criminal Procedure Code. The lawfulness of detention beyond a day sits in section 117. Whether a statement made to a police officer may be used at all sits in a separate provision of the Code."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Hafiz's statement turns out to be accurate in every detail. He was still promised leniency by the investigating officer. What follows?",options:["The statement goes in, because accuracy cures the promise","The judge admits it and gives it less weight","The statement may still be shut out, because voluntariness is the entry test"],answer:2,why:"Truth is not the test for getting a confession in. One caused by a promise of advantage from a person in authority is kept out, however accurate it later proves. Giving evidence less weight happens after admission, and that stage is never reached."},{t:"p",text:"A retracted confession is a separate problem. The accused says in court that he never meant it, and the court then decides what the retraction is worth."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-expert-opinion",moduleId:"m10-evidence",title:"When the court will listen to an opinion",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A witness is there to say what happened, not what they think it means. Expert opinion is the exception to that, and it is narrower than most people expect.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 (Act 56), the opinion evidence provisions in Part I, including the fields in which the opinion of a specially skilled person is made relevant and the provision making the grounds of an opinion relevant.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for expert opinion, for the grounds of an opinion, or for opinion on handwriting by a person acquainted with it on purpose; confirm each in the current Evidence Act 1950 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"evidence-act-1950",where:"the run of opinion evidence sections in Part I, from the expert provision to the end of that group"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-expert-opinion-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Puan Latifah says the signature on the transfer is not hers. Nobody in court can settle that by looking.",h:"A signature nobody agrees about",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Latifah's land in Alor Setar has been transferred to her nephew. She says she never signed the transfer form."},{t:"p",text:"The signature on the form looks like hers. It also looks like three others in the same file. The judge cannot settle this by staring at it."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Who may tell the court whether that signature is genuine?",options:["Only a document examiner with the relevant skill","Any witness who has an honest view about it","Only Puan Latifah, because it is her own signature"],answer:0,reveal:"A qualified document examiner, and that is the main route. Latifah may of course say the signature is not hers, but she is a party with an interest. The Act also allows a person genuinely familiar with someone's handwriting to speak to it, which is a narrow second route."},{t:"p",text:"That is the shape of the subject. Most witnesses may not offer a view at all. A few may."}]},{id:"sec-l-expert-opinion-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The court draws the conclusions, and a witness who offers one is doing the judge's job with less information.",h:"Why opinions are usually kept out",body:[{t:"p",text:"The ordinary rule is short. A witness states the facts they perceived. Drawing conclusions from those facts is the court's work."},{t:"p",text:"There are two reasons for that, and both are practical."},{t:"list",items:["**The judge decides the case.** Letting witnesses vote on the outcome does not make the decision better, only louder.","**An opinion cannot be tested the way a fact can.** You can ask a witness where she stood. You cannot cross-examine a hunch."]},{t:"p",text:"So a neighbour may say she saw the car swerve across the line. She may not say the driver was negligent."},{t:"caution",text:"The line is thinner than it looks. Ordinary witnesses give compressed judgments constantly, about speed, about age, about whether someone seemed drunk. Those are treated as shorthand for what was observed."}]},{id:"sec-l-expert-opinion-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Skill in a listed field, proved to the court, on a question the court cannot answer without help.",h:"What makes a witness an expert",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Act makes relevant the opinions of persons specially skilled in certain matters. The listed fields are where you start."},{t:"list",items:["Foreign law.","Science.","Art.","The identity of handwriting.","Finger impressions."]},{t:"p",text:"Skill is proved, not assumed. The party calling the expert has to show the court where that skill came from."},{t:"p",text:"It can come from a degree, from a professional qualification, or from years of doing the work. Experience on its own can be enough."},{t:"example",text:"Latifah calls a document examiner with fifteen years in a government laboratory. The nephew objects that she holds no university degree. That objection is weak, because skill can be built by practice."},{t:"caution",text:"An expert is an expert only on their own field. A fire investigator asked to give a view on a company's accounts is a lay witness with an impressive title."}]},{id:"sec-l-expert-opinion-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Admitting an opinion is not accepting it, and an opinion with no stated reasoning is worth very little.",h:"Getting it in is only half the job",body:[{t:"p",text:"Getting an expert's evidence in is the first half. What the court does with it is the second, and answers usually skip that part."},{t:"p",text:"The court is not bound to accept the opinion. It weighs the opinion like any other piece of evidence."},{t:"p",text:"What gives an expert weight is the reasoning behind the conclusion. An expert who states a conclusion and no grounds has given the court nothing to check."},{t:"p",text:"The Act treats the grounds of an opinion as relevant as well. That is the statutory expression of the same idea."},{t:"table",caption:"What separates a strong expert opinion from a weak one",columns:["Strong","Weak"],rows:[["Sets out the method used, step by step","States a conclusion and stops"],["Names the material actually examined","Relies on documents nobody produced"],["Says how confident the conclusion is","Speaks in flat certainties"],["Answers the technical question only","Tells the court who should win"]],alt:"A table contrasting strong and weak expert opinion. A strong opinion sets out its method step by step, names the material actually examined, says how confident it is, and answers only the technical question. A weak one states a bare conclusion, relies on documents nobody produced, speaks in flat certainties, and tells the court who should win."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Two document examiners reach opposite conclusions about Latifah's signature. What should the judge do?",options:["Prefer whichever expert has more years of experience","Examine the reasoning each gave and decide which holds up","Treat the point as unproved and put both aside"],answer:1,why:"The court weighs expert evidence like any other evidence, and reasoning is what makes it weighable. Length of service is one factor and never the deciding one. Putting both aside would leave a fact in issue undecided when material to decide it was given."},{t:"caution",text:"Watch for an expert who answers the very question the court has to decide. That is where an opinion stops helping the judgment and starts replacing it."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-witness-examination",moduleId:"m10-evidence",title:"Three turns at a witness, and what a witness may refuse",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A witness is questioned three times over, and each turn has its own rules about what may be asked. A witness may also refuse some questions outright, and the widest refusal protects what a client told a lawyer.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 (Act 56), the provisions on the examination of witnesses and ss 126 to 129 including the proviso to s 126; Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), s 180.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for examination-in-chief, cross-examination, re-examination or leading questions on purpose; confirm each in the current Evidence Act 1950 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Sections 126 to 129 are stated in substance rather than word for word, so read them in the current text as well.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"evidence-act-1950",where:"sections 126 to 129 word for word, proviso included, then the chapter on the examination of witnesses"},{statuteId:"criminal-procedure-code",where:"section 180, which is the checkpoint the prosecution's witnesses are ultimately measured against"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-witness-examination-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Cik Nurul is questioned by three people in turn, under three different sets of rules.",h:"Cik Nurul in the box",body:[{t:"p",text:"Cik Nurul saw a car crash on Jalan Tun Abdul Razak. She is called by the plaintiff in the Sessions Court in Johor Bahru."},{t:"p",text:"Her time in the box has three parts. The plaintiff's lawyer asks first. Then the defendant's lawyer. Then the plaintiff's lawyer again, briefly."},{t:"predict",prompt:"The plaintiff's lawyer opens with: the blue car ran the red light, didn't it? Is that allowed?",options:["Yes, he called her, so he may lead her","No, you may not lead your own witness on a disputed point","Only if the defendant's lawyer does not object"],answer:1,reveal:"Not allowed. A leading question suggests the answer, and you may not feed answers to your own witness on the disputed part of the case. Leading is fine for uncontested background, and it is the normal way to cross-examine. Objection matters in practice, but it is not the reason."},{t:"p",text:"Each turn has its own name and its own limits. Running them together is the most visible mistake in a moot."}]},{id:"sec-l-witness-examination-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Examination-in-chief, cross-examination, re-examination, in that order, and only the middle one lets you lead.",h:"The three turns",body:[{t:"p",text:"The three stages run in a fixed order. Each one is limited by what came before it."},{t:"table",caption:"The three stages of questioning a witness",columns:["Stage","Who asks","Leading questions","What it may cover"],rows:[["Examination-in-chief","The party who called the witness","Not on disputed matters","The witness's own account"],["Cross-examination","The opposing party","Allowed","Anything relevant, including credit"],["Re-examination","The party who called the witness","Not on disputed matters","Explaining what came out in cross-examination"]],alt:"A table of the three stages of questioning a witness. Examination-in-chief is taken by the party who called the witness, may not lead on disputed matters, and covers the witness's own account. Cross-examination is taken by the opposing party, may lead, and covers anything relevant including credit. Re-examination returns to the party who called the witness, may not lead, and is confined to explaining what came out in cross-examination."},{t:"p",text:"A **leading question** is one that suggests the answer wanted. Did you see the broken bottle is leading. What did you see is not."},{t:"p",text:"Re-examination is the narrowest stage. It exists to repair damage done in cross-examination. It may not open new ground without leave of the court."},{t:"caution",text:"Re-examination is not a second chance to make your case. A question there with no root in the cross-examination is objectionable, and it will be stopped."}]},{id:"sec-l-witness-examination-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"If you never challenge a witness's account, you cannot later ask the court to disbelieve it.",h:"Cross-examination, and the duty to put your case",body:[{t:"p",text:"Cross-examination is where the four risks tested in the hearsay lesson are actually attacked. Perception, memory, honesty, and choice of words."},{t:"p",text:"It has a second job that answers often miss. You have to put your own case to the witness."},{t:"p",text:"That means telling the witness what your client says happened. It means giving them the chance to accept it or deny it."},{t:"example",text:"The defendant's case is that Nurul stood behind a bus and saw nothing. His lawyer must put that to her. Saying it for the first time in closing submissions is too late."},{t:"caution",text:"Failing to put your case invites the court to treat that witness's account as unchallenged. It is a common and expensive mistake, and it cannot be repaired afterwards."},{t:"p",text:"When the prosecution has finished with all its witnesses, a criminal trial reaches a fixed checkpoint."},{t:"rule",text:"At the close of the case for the prosecution the court shall consider whether the prosecution has made out a prima facie case. If it has not, the court shall record an order of acquittal. If it has, the court shall call upon the accused to enter his defence.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code, s 180"},{t:"p",text:"So the value of cross-examination is tested there first. A case taken apart in cross-examination may never reach a defence at all."}]},{id:"sec-l-witness-examination-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"What a client told a lawyer is protected, and the protection belongs to the client rather than to the lawyer.",h:"What a witness may refuse to answer",body:[{t:"p",text:"A witness generally answers. Privilege is the narrow set of questions a witness may refuse, and the widest of them covers legal advice."},{t:"rule",text:"An advocate shall not be permitted to disclose any communication made to him in the course and for the purpose of his employment as an advocate, or the contents of any document he became acquainted with in that course, or any advice given by him to his client.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 126"},{t:"p",text:"Two things escape that protection. Both are set out in the proviso to the same section."},{t:"list",items:["A communication made in furtherance of an illegal purpose.","A fact observed by the advocate in the course of the employment, showing that a crime or fraud has been committed since the employment began."]},{t:"rule",text:"The protection in section 126 extends to interpreters and to the clerks and servants of an advocate.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 127"},{t:"rule",text:"No one shall be compelled to disclose to the court any confidential communication between himself and his legal professional adviser, unless he offers himself as a witness.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 129"},{t:"table",caption:"Where each of the four privilege provisions bites",columns:["Provision","What it does"],rows:[["s 126","Stops the advocate disclosing what the client told him"],["s 127","Extends that to interpreters, clerks and servants"],["s 128","Says when a party is taken to have consented to disclosure"],["s 129","Stops the client being compelled to disclose the same material"]],alt:"A table of the four privilege provisions. Section 126 stops the advocate disclosing what the client told him. Section 127 extends that protection to interpreters, clerks and servants. Section 128 says when a party is taken to have consented to disclosure. Section 129 stops the client being compelled to disclose the same material."},{t:"caution",text:"The privilege belongs to the client, not to the lawyer. A lawyer cannot waive it to be helpful. A client who simply gives evidence in the case has not waived it either."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A client tells his advocate how he plans to forge a receipt he will rely on at trial. Is that communication protected?",options:["Yes, because everything said to an advocate is protected","Yes, unless the advocate reports it to someone","No, because the proviso removes a communication made in furtherance of an illegal purpose"],answer:2,why:"The proviso to section 126 takes out communications made in furtherance of an illegal purpose. The protection exists for getting legal advice, not for arranging a fraud. Whether the advocate must report anything is a separate professional question."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-burden",moduleId:"m10-evidence",title:"Burden and standard of proof under the Evidence Act",minutes:25,summary:"Sections 101 to 106 distribute the burden. They separate the burden of proof from the burden of adducing evidence, and a great deal turns on which one a provision is talking about.",plants:["ms-ev-01","ms-ev-02","ms-ev-03","ms-ev-04","ms-ev-05"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"malaysian-law-of-evidence-student",where:"the chapters on burden of proof and presumptions"},{bookId:"mazlina-law-of-evidence",where:"for the treatment of ss 101-106"}],source:"Evidence Act 1950 ss 4, 5, 101, 102, 103, 105, 106 and 114 illustration (g).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check the sections against the current text of Act 56, and check current authority on the conditions for drawing an adverse inference under s 114(g) — it is frequently argued and the formulations differ.",plate:"balance",plateCaption:"The two standards on one beam. Section 105 is the main statutory exception to the rule that the prosecution proves everything.",readMinutes:9,sections:[{id:"sec-l-burden-0",readMinutes:4,keypoint:"Section 101 is the legal burden and it does not shift; section 103 is the evidential one and it does.",anchor:"fig-l-burden-0",h:"Two different burdens",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two people give different accounts of the same events and there is no CCTV. Somebody has to lose. The Evidence Act 1950 decides who."},{t:"p",text:"Section 101 sets out the basic idea and gives it a name."},{t:"rule",text:"Whoever desires any court to give judgment as to any legal right or liability, dependent on the existence of facts which he asserts, must prove that those facts exist. When a person is bound to prove the existence of any fact, it is said that the burden of proof lies on that person.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 101"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: if you want the court to act on a fact, it is your job to prove it. That job is called the burden of proof."},{t:"p",text:"Section 102 answers a different question. Who is carrying that burden in this particular case?"},{t:"rule",text:"The burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 102"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: imagine that neither side calls a single witness. Whoever loses in that imaginary case is the one carrying the burden."},{t:"p",text:"What sections 101 and 102 describe is the legal burden. That is the risk of losing if the fact is not proved, and it does not shift."},{t:"p",text:"Section 103 is doing something else. It places the burden of proving any particular fact on the person who wishes the court to believe it."},{t:"p",text:"That is the evidential burden — the burden of putting enough evidence on a point to make it live. Unlike the legal burden, it does shift as the case proceeds."},{t:"example",text:"Puan Chin sues over an unpaid loan of RM8,000. If neither side calls any evidence she loses, so the legal burden is hers. She produces the bank transfer and a message promising repayment. In practice the defendant must now answer. That is the evidential burden moving, not the legal one."},{t:"caution",text:"Answers routinely say 'the burden shifted to the defendant' when they mean the evidential burden. Say which one you mean. The legal burden in a criminal case shifts only where a statute makes it shift."},{t:"table",caption:"Which burden a provision is talking about",columns:[{label:"Provision"},{label:"Burden"},{label:"Does it shift?"}],rows:[["s 101 — whoever asserts must prove","Legal","No"],["s 102 — who would fail on a blank record","Legal — identifies who carries it","No"],["s 103 — proving any particular fact","Evidential","Yes, as the evidence goes in"],["s 105 — a general exception","Legal, **on the accused**, on the balance of probabilities","Placed there by statute"],["s 106 — a fact especially within knowledge","That specific fact only","Cannot reverse the general burden"]],source:"Evidence Act 1950 ss 101-106"},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Puan Chin produces the bank transfer and the message promising repayment. In practice the defendant must now answer. Which burden has moved?",options:["The legal burden — the defendant must now prove he repaid","Both, because she has proved her case","The evidential burden — the legal burden stays with her"],answer:2,why:"The legal burden is the risk of losing if a fact is not proved, and it does not shift. What moved is the practical need to answer. Writing 'the burden shifted' without saying which one is the routine error."}]},{id:"sec-l-burden-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Section 105 puts a real legal burden on an accused relying on a general exception.",h:"Section 105 and the exceptions",body:[{t:"p",text:"Section 105 is one of those statutes, and it was met in the criminal law module."},{t:"p",text:"It places on the accused the burden of proving circumstances that bring the case within a general exception in the Penal Code. It does the same for any special exception or proviso in the law defining the offence. It also directs the court to presume the absence of those circumstances."},{t:"p",text:"That is a genuine legal burden on the accused, discharged on the balance of probabilities. It is the principal statutory exception to the rule that the prosecution proves everything."}]},{id:"sec-l-burden-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Section 106 reaches one fact peculiarly within someone's knowledge — it cannot reverse the case.",h:"Facts only one person can know",body:[{t:"p",text:"A man is found riding a motorcycle that was reported stolen. Asked where he got it, he says nothing. Who has to prove what?"},{t:"predict",prompt:"The motorcycle was reported stolen and he is riding it. He will not say where he got it. Who has to prove where it came from?",options:["The prosecution — it has to prove its whole case","The rider, because that fact sits with him alone","Nobody; saying nothing proves nothing either way"],answer:1,reveal:"Section 106 puts it on the rider. Where a fact is known to one person and nobody else, that person has to prove it. Keep the first answer too. It stays true of the case as a whole, and the caution below is about how often section 106 is stretched past this point."},{t:"rule",text:"When any fact is especially within the knowledge of any person, the burden of proving that fact is upon him.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 106"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: where a fact sits with one person and with nobody else, that person has to prove it."},{t:"p",text:"The point of the section is practical. A licence, an authority, a reason for being somewhere — these sit with one person. It would be unreasonable to make the other side prove a negative about them."},{t:"caution",text:"Section 106 is the most over-used provision in the Act. It does not relieve the prosecution of proving its case. It applies to a specific fact peculiarly within a person's knowledge, and it cannot be used to reverse the general burden. An answer that deploys s 106 to shift the whole case has misread it."}]},{id:"sec-l-burden-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"May presume, shall presume, conclusive proof: three different strengths, and the Act picks deliberately.",h:"Presumptions, and section 114",body:[{t:"p",text:"A presumption is a direction about what the court may or must infer. Section 4 defines three strengths of it."},{t:"list",items:["**May presume** — the court has a discretion. It may treat the fact as proved, or it may call for proof of it.","**Shall presume** — the court must treat the fact as proved, unless and until it is disproved.","**Conclusive proof** — the fact is settled, and no evidence to the contrary is allowed."]},{t:"p",text:"Section 114 lists facts the court may presume. Illustration (g) is the one that appears constantly. Evidence which could be produced and is not would, if produced, be unfavourable to the person who withholds it."},{t:"example",text:"A driver's dashcam was working on the day of the crash, and he does not produce the footage. The other side asks the court to infer that it would not have helped him. That is illustration (g)."},{t:"caution",text:"Illustration (g) is permissive, and it is not drawn automatically from every gap in the evidence. The party inviting the inference must show that the evidence was available and material, and that no explanation for its absence has been given."}]},{id:"sec-l-burden-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"None of it arises until the evidence is admissible, and relevance is defined by the Act.",h:"Relevancy is still the gate",body:[{t:"p",text:"None of this arises until the evidence is admissible. Section 5 admits evidence of facts in issue and of relevant facts, and relevance is defined by the Act rather than at large."},{t:"p",text:"A fact is not admissible because it is interesting, or because it sounds probative, or because it seems fair to admit it. It is admissible because a section makes it relevant."},{t:"p",text:"Keep the two questions apart in writing. First: is this admissible under a named section? Second: who has to prove what, and to what standard? Merging them is the fastest way to a confused answer."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qbn1",q:"Which section says the burden lies on the person who would fail if no evidence were given?",options:["Section 101","Section 102","Section 103","Section 106"],answer:1,why:"Section 102 identifies who carries the legal burden by asking who loses on a blank record.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 102"},{id:"qbn2",q:"What is the effect of s 105 on an accused relying on a general exception?",options:["He carries an evidential burden only","The prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt","The exception is presumed in his favour","He carries a legal burden, on the balance of probabilities"],answer:3,why:"The court presumes the circumstances are absent, and the accused must prove them.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 105"},{id:"qbn3",q:"What is the correct scope of s 106?",options:["A specific fact especially within a person's knowledge","The whole of the defence case","Any fact the prosecution cannot easily prove","All facts occurring in private"],answer:0,why:"One specific fact peculiarly within a person's knowledge. It cannot be used to reverse the general burden.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 106"},{id:"qbn4",q:"What does 'shall presume' mean under s 4?",options:["The court must treat the fact as proved unless it is disproved","The court may treat the fact as proved","No evidence in rebuttal is allowed","The fact is admitted"],answer:0,why:"'May presume' is a discretion, and 'conclusive proof' admits no rebuttal at all.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 4"},{id:"qbn5",q:"When may an adverse inference be drawn under illustration (g) to s 114?",options:["From any gap in the evidence","Whenever a witness is not called","Automatically against the defence","Where material evidence was available, withheld, and unexplained"],answer:3,why:"It is permissive and conditional, not a default the court applies to every gap.",source:"Evidence Act 1950 s 114 illustration (g)"},{id:"qbn6",q:"What must be established before any question of burden arises?",options:["That the witness is credible","That the parties agree","That the fact is important","That the evidence is admissible under a named section"],answer:3,why:"Relevancy is the gate, and it is defined by the Act rather than left at large.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, Part I; relevancy is the gate"}]}];export{e as default};
